From 898a600991e369bb1ce6197aef6c78bfb287de4c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Valery Petrov <valerypetrov@icloud.com>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 12:55:59 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Refuse remote-write metadata a Distributed table has no
 column to carry

A Distributed table over per-shard TimeSeries tables need not declare every
outer column a TimeSeries table has: one declaring metric_name, tags and
time_series carries samples, and the tests use that shape. Metric metadata is
stored in four columns of its own, and makeMetricsMetadataBlock read all four
off the table the handler names, so a sender that put metadata in its
WriteRequest got NO_SUCH_COLUMN_IN_TABLE from a table that looked fine for
samples.

Building those columns anyway would not help: the sink sends exactly the
columns the table declares, so metadata it cannot name could never reach a
shard that would store it. Such a request is refused instead, before the
shards are asked anything, with INCOMPATIBLE_SCHEMA naming the missing column
and what to declare. A TimeSeries table always has the four, so only a
narrow wrapper is affected, and a request without metadata is untouched.

The test sends one sample and its family's metadata in the one request a
sender sends both in: through a wrapper of the full schema the metadata
reaches the shards, through the narrow one the request is refused whole with
nothing left on either shard, and the same samples without metadata still
travel the narrow wrapper.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_016i1XnLC3KypZdaYYsfzqub
---
 .../features/interfaces/prometheus.mdx        |  2 +-
 .../PrometheusRemoteWriteProtocol.cpp         | 28 +++++++-
 .../test_write_distributed.py                 | 65 +++++++++++++++++++
 3 files changed, 93 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/docs/concepts/features/interfaces/prometheus.mdx b/docs/concepts/features/interfaces/prometheus.mdx
index 9c9e0b360ce..b0c304a2e4b 100644
--- a/docs/concepts/features/interfaces/prometheus.mdx
+++ b/docs/concepts/features/interfaces/prometheus.mdx
@@ -86,7 +86,7 @@ ClickHouse implements the Prometheus HTTP API over a [`TimeSeries`](/reference/e
 
 The query endpoints, remote write, and the `prometheusQuery`/`prometheusQueryRange` table functions also accept a `Distributed` table over per-shard `TimeSeries` tables: samples are selected on every shard and the PromQL is evaluated on the initiator, and writes are routed through the `Distributed` sink. Remote read and the metadata endpoints (`/api/v1/series`, `/api/v1/labels`, `/api/v1/label/<name>/values`, `/api/v1/metadata`) remain `TimeSeries`-only and refuse a `Distributed` target.
 
-Every shard-local table behind such a `Distributed` table must be a `TimeSeries` table declaring the same `time_series` type as the `Distributed` table itself; a mismatch is refused rather than rounded, and the check asks every replica of every shard on every request, so a shard-local table swapped for another under the same name is caught by the next request rather than read. The check asks each replica with `SHOW CREATE TABLE` and `DESC TABLE`, which need only `SHOW COLUMNS` on that table, a right any grant on it implies, so it reads nothing of `system` on the shard and a row policy or filter there cannot blind it; a replica that cannot answer at all is reported as unverified rather than failing the request. A replica that refuses to answer it, because the cluster's user holds only the column-level `INSERT` the write itself needs, is left to the check its own `INSERT` makes on the target it resolves, so the check never asks for a right the write does not. Remote write also sets `insert_expected_table_engine` to `TimeSeries` and `insert_expected_column_types` to the `time_series` type the table declares on the `INSERT` it dispatches, so a shard-local table swapped after that check, whether for another engine or for a `TimeSeries` table of another type, is refused where the `INSERT` resolves it on the shard, rather than taking the batch and having the sink convert the samples into it; the write then fails with a retryable `5xx`, Prometheus resends it, and the next check refuses it. A read is not failed by a replica the check cannot reach or that has no table of that name: whether the read itself fails is decided, as before, by its own `skip_unavailable_shards` settings. Remote write, however, is refused (with a retryable `5xx`) while any replica is unreachable, has no table of that name, or does not show that table's `time_series` type, because a replica the check could not see would still receive the samples unchecked. When the `Distributed` table declares no database, each shard resolves the name in its own default database, except a shard that is this server itself, which is always read and written in-process (`prefer_localhost_replica` is pinned on for both, and parallel replicas off for the reads) and so resolves it in the requesting user's current database, exactly as any query here would; the check resolves that shard's table in-process on the requesting user's context too, so the cluster's connection credentials play no part for it, and the write goes to the table so resolved, as any query keeps the tables it resolved even if a concurrent `RENAME` or `EXCHANGE` moves the name, so long as that table's database assigns UUIDs (`Atomic`, `Replicated`); where it does not, the write re-resolves the name and is refused, as on any other shard. Reading through it needs `SELECT` on the `Distributed` table and `READ ON REMOTE`, because the query is rewritten into a `cluster()` call; both are checked before the shard-local check above runs, so a caller without either is refused with a plain access error rather than told what the shards hold. The `prometheusQuery`/`prometheusQueryRange` table functions need `CREATE TEMPORARY TABLE` on top of those, as they do over a `TimeSeries` table; the query endpoints name the selector inside the `cluster()` call for each shard to resolve, so they need it only for a shard that is this server itself, together with `SELECT` on that shard's table; both are checked before the shard-local check runs too. Writing needs `INSERT` on the `Distributed` table and, whenever the cluster has a shard that is this server itself, on that shard's own table with the columns the sink sends, whether or not the batch routes any sample there; that one is checked before the shard-local check too.
+Every shard-local table behind such a `Distributed` table must be a `TimeSeries` table declaring the same `time_series` type as the `Distributed` table itself; a mismatch is refused rather than rounded, and the check asks every replica of every shard on every request, so a shard-local table swapped for another under the same name is caught by the next request rather than read. The check asks each replica with `SHOW CREATE TABLE` and `DESC TABLE`, which need only `SHOW COLUMNS` on that table, a right any grant on it implies, so it reads nothing of `system` on the shard and a row policy or filter there cannot blind it; a replica that cannot answer at all is reported as unverified rather than failing the request. A replica that refuses to answer it, because the cluster's user holds only the column-level `INSERT` the write itself needs, is left to the check its own `INSERT` makes on the target it resolves, so the check never asks for a right the write does not. Remote write also sets `insert_expected_table_engine` to `TimeSeries` and `insert_expected_column_types` to the `time_series` type the table declares on the `INSERT` it dispatches, so a shard-local table swapped after that check, whether for another engine or for a `TimeSeries` table of another type, is refused where the `INSERT` resolves it on the shard, rather than taking the batch and having the sink convert the samples into it; the write then fails with a retryable `5xx`, Prometheus resends it, and the next check refuses it. A read is not failed by a replica the check cannot reach or that has no table of that name: whether the read itself fails is decided, as before, by its own `skip_unavailable_shards` settings. Remote write, however, is refused (with a retryable `5xx`) while any replica is unreachable, has no table of that name, or does not show that table's `time_series` type, because a replica the check could not see would still receive the samples unchecked. When the `Distributed` table declares no database, each shard resolves the name in its own default database, except a shard that is this server itself, which is always read and written in-process (`prefer_localhost_replica` is pinned on for both, and parallel replicas off for the reads) and so resolves it in the requesting user's current database, exactly as any query here would; the check resolves that shard's table in-process on the requesting user's context too, so the cluster's connection credentials play no part for it, and the write goes to the table so resolved, as any query keeps the tables it resolved even if a concurrent `RENAME` or `EXCHANGE` moves the name, so long as that table's database assigns UUIDs (`Atomic`, `Replicated`); where it does not, the write re-resolves the name and is refused, as on any other shard. Reading through it needs `SELECT` on the `Distributed` table and `READ ON REMOTE`, because the query is rewritten into a `cluster()` call; both are checked before the shard-local check above runs, so a caller without either is refused with a plain access error rather than told what the shards hold. The `prometheusQuery`/`prometheusQueryRange` table functions need `CREATE TEMPORARY TABLE` on top of those, as they do over a `TimeSeries` table; the query endpoints name the selector inside the `cluster()` call for each shard to resolve, so they need it only for a shard that is this server itself, together with `SELECT` on that shard's table; both are checked before the shard-local check runs too. Writing needs `INSERT` on the `Distributed` table and, whenever the cluster has a shard that is this server itself, on that shard's own table with the columns the sink sends, whether or not the batch routes any sample there; that one is checked before the shard-local check too. The `Distributed` table need not declare every outer column of a `TimeSeries` table: one declaring only `metric_name`, `tags` and `time_series` carries samples. It cannot carry metric metadata, though, because the sink sends exactly the columns the table declares, so a remote write that brings metadata is refused up front unless the table also declares `metric_family`, `type`, `unit` and `help`.
 
 Remote write into a `Distributed` target is an `INSERT` through that table's sink with [`distributed_foreground_insert`](/reference/settings/session-settings/distributed#distributed_foreground_insert) forced on and [`async_insert`](/reference/settings/session-settings/async-insert#async_insert) forced off, whatever the handler's user profile or the `remote_write` URL says: the samples are not queued on the initiator, by the sink or by the asynchronous insert queue, but delivered to the shards before the response, so a `204` means every shard (one replica per shard under `internal_replication`) has accepted them, and a shard that fails the insert fails the write with a retryable `5xx`, which Prometheus answers by resending the batch. A batch left in either queue would be flushed after the check above, into whatever answers to the shard-local name by then, which is why background delivery is not offered on this path. [`skip_unavailable_shards`](/reference/settings/session-settings/skip-unavailable-shards#skip_unavailable_shards) is forced off on this path too, whatever the URL or the profile says: a shard the sink skipped would be samples dropped under a `204`. The routing settings `insert_shard_id` and `insert_distributed_one_random_shard` are refused on this path, whether they come from the URL or from the profile: a batch goes where the table's sharding key sends it, so a `Distributed` table with several shards and no key cannot be written through remote write.
 
diff --git a/src/Storages/TimeSeries/PrometheusRemoteWriteProtocol.cpp b/src/Storages/TimeSeries/PrometheusRemoteWriteProtocol.cpp
index da9755e7c94..190d00a053e 100644
--- a/src/Storages/TimeSeries/PrometheusRemoteWriteProtocol.cpp
+++ b/src/Storages/TimeSeries/PrometheusRemoteWriteProtocol.cpp
@@ -50,6 +50,7 @@ namespace ErrorCodes
     extern const int ASYNC_INSERT_FLUSH_TIMEOUT;
     extern const int ILLEGAL_COLUMN;
     extern const int ILLEGAL_TIME_SERIES_TAGS;
+    extern const int INCOMPATIBLE_SCHEMA;
     extern const int LOGICAL_ERROR;
 }
 
@@ -176,6 +177,27 @@ Block makeTimeSeriesBlock(
     return block;
 }
 
+/// Metadata is stored in columns of its own, which a Distributed table declaring only the sample columns has not got:
+/// the sink sends what the wrapper declares, so metadata it cannot name could not reach a shard that would store it.
+void checkTableAcceptsMetricsMetadata(const StorageInMemoryMetadata & metadata, const StorageID & storage_id)
+{
+    for (const auto * column :
+         {TimeSeriesColumnNames::MetricFamily, TimeSeriesColumnNames::Type, TimeSeriesColumnNames::Unit, TimeSeriesColumnNames::Help})
+    {
+        if (!metadata.columns.has(column))
+            throw Exception(
+                ErrorCodes::INCOMPATIBLE_SCHEMA,
+                "Table {} does not declare column `{}`, so remote write cannot store the metric metadata sent with the samples: "
+                "declare `{}`, `{}`, `{}` and `{}` on it, or send the samples without metadata",
+                storage_id.getNameForLogs(),
+                column,
+                TimeSeriesColumnNames::MetricFamily,
+                TimeSeriesColumnNames::Type,
+                TimeSeriesColumnNames::Unit,
+                TimeSeriesColumnNames::Help);
+    }
+}
+
 Block makeMetricsMetadataBlock(
     const google::protobuf::RepeatedPtrField<prometheus::MetricMetadata> & metrics_metadata,
     size_t num_time_series_rows,
@@ -353,11 +375,15 @@ void PrometheusRemoteWriteProtocol::write(
         time_series.size(),
         metrics_metadata.size());
 
+    auto metadata = time_series_storage->getInMemoryMetadataPtr(getContext(), false);
+    /// Refused before the shards are asked anything: no wrapper of that shape could take this request.
+    if (!metrics_metadata.empty())
+        checkTableAcceptsMetricsMetadata(*metadata, storage_id);
+
     /// The sink would accept shard targets no prometheus read surface can answer from, and a caller's
     /// own shard choice; checked here, not on construction, with no request body read in between.
     checkPrometheusQueryDistributedWrite(*time_series_storage, getContext());
 
-    auto metadata = time_series_storage->getInMemoryMetadataPtr(getContext(), false);
     FailPointInjection::pauseFailPoint(FailPoints::prometheus_remote_write_before_insert);
     insertBlock(makeBlock(time_series, metrics_metadata, *metadata), *time_series_storage, getContext());
 
diff --git a/tests/integration/test_prometheus_protocols/test_write_distributed.py b/tests/integration/test_prometheus_protocols/test_write_distributed.py
index e508c5b70e2..5e549ab5e74 100644
--- a/tests/integration/test_prometheus_protocols/test_write_distributed.py
+++ b/tests/integration/test_prometheus_protocols/test_write_distributed.py
@@ -6,6 +6,7 @@ from helpers.cluster import ClickHouseCluster
 from helpers.test_tools import assert_eq_with_retry
 
 from .prometheus_test_utils import (
+    convert_metrics_metadata_to_protobuf,
     convert_time_series_to_protobuf,
     execute_query_via_http_api,
     get_response_to_remote_write,
@@ -45,6 +46,12 @@ CLUSTER_COLUMN_USER = "prom_cluster_column_user"
 # The columns a remote write sends, and so the only ones the shard INSERT names.
 WRITTEN_COLUMNS = "metric_name, tags, time_series"
 
+# The metric family a sample and its metadata are sent under, and the metadata sent with it: a
+# wrapper declaring only the columns above has no column any of the three could travel in.
+METADATA_METRIC = "metadata_metric"
+METADATA_HELP = "Metadata carried with the samples"
+METADATA_UNIT = "seconds"
+
 
 @pytest.fixture(scope="module", autouse=True)
 def start_cluster():
@@ -174,6 +181,19 @@ def write_one(path, metric_name, host, timestamp):
     )
 
 
+def write_with_metadata(path, metric_name):
+    """One sample and the metadata of its family, in the one request a sender sends both in."""
+    write_request = convert_time_series_to_protobuf(
+        [({"__name__": metric_name, "host": "h0"}, {START_TIME: 1.0})]
+    )
+    write_request.metadata.extend(
+        convert_metrics_metadata_to_protobuf(
+            [(metric_name, "GAUGE", METADATA_HELP, METADATA_UNIT)]
+        ).metadata
+    )
+    return get_response_to_remote_write(node.ip_address, 9093, path, write_request)
+
+
 def count_on_the_shards(wrapper, metric_name, flush=True, table="ts_local"):
     if flush:
         node.query(f"SYSTEM FLUSH DISTRIBUTED {wrapper}")
@@ -185,6 +205,18 @@ def count_on_the_shards(wrapper, metric_name, flush=True, table="ts_local"):
     )
 
 
+def metadata_on_the_shards(metric_family, table="ts_local"):
+    """The `type`, `help` and `unit` stored for a metric family, from both shards: a metadata row
+    carries no tags, so the sharding key always puts it on one of the two."""
+    return node.query(
+        f"SELECT type, help, unit FROM timeSeriesMetrics(shard_0.{table})"
+        f" WHERE metric_family_name = '{metric_family}'"
+        " UNION ALL "
+        f"SELECT type, help, unit FROM timeSeriesMetrics(shard_1.{table})"
+        f" WHERE metric_family_name = '{metric_family}'"
+    )
+
+
 def test_remote_write_rejects_non_timeseries_shards():
     """The wrapper declares no remote database, so each shard resolves `mt_bad` in its own default
     database - the case the initiator cannot answer with its own `currentDatabase()`."""
@@ -493,3 +525,36 @@ def test_the_probe_accepts_a_cluster_user_granted_only_the_written_columns():
         ).strip()
         == "0"
     )
+
+
+def test_remote_write_metadata_needs_a_wrapper_declaring_its_columns():
+    """Metadata reaches the shards through a wrapper of the full schema; the same request over one
+    declaring only the sample columns is refused whole, since none of them could carry it."""
+    # The full wrapper: the sample and the metadata of its family both reach the shard tables.
+    response = write_with_metadata("/dist/write", METADATA_METRIC)
+    assert response.status_code == 204, response.text
+    assert count_on_the_shards("prom_dist", METADATA_METRIC) == 1
+    stored = metadata_on_the_shards(METADATA_METRIC)
+    assert stored == f"gauge\t{METADATA_HELP}\t{METADATA_UNIT}\n", stored
+
+    # The same request over a wrapper declaring only the columns a sample needs is refused before
+    # the shards are asked anything, and the refusal names the column the metadata would need.
+    response = write_with_metadata("/column_granted/write", METADATA_METRIC)
+    assert response.status_code >= 500, response.text
+    assert "INCOMPATIBLE_SCHEMA" in response.text
+    assert "prom_column_granted" in response.text
+    assert "does not declare column `metric_family`" in response.text
+    # Nothing landed on either shard: neither the samples nor the metadata sent with them.
+    on_the_narrow_shards = count_on_the_shards(
+        "prom_column_granted", METADATA_METRIC, table="ts_column_granted"
+    )
+    assert on_the_narrow_shards == 0
+    assert metadata_on_the_shards(METADATA_METRIC, table="ts_column_granted") == ""
+
+    # Not vacuous: the very same samples without metadata still travel that narrow wrapper.
+    assert write("/column_granted/write", METADATA_METRIC).status_code == 204
+    on_the_narrow_shards = count_on_the_shards(
+        "prom_column_granted", METADATA_METRIC, table="ts_column_granted"
+    )
+    assert on_the_narrow_shards == 1
+    assert metadata_on_the_shards(METADATA_METRIC, table="ts_column_granted") == ""
